# none). WirePlumber's ACP path reads /usr/share/alsa/ucm2 to build a card's
# profiles; without it a plain HDA card only gets a generic Analog profile and
# the monitor's HDMI speakers never appear. Kept after the 2026-10-06 removal of
# the board overlay's 51-alsa-ucm.conf split-mode rule (api.alsa.use-acp=false):
# that rule bypassed ACP and UCM both, and its split-mode HDMI sink was silent on
# the N100. Under stock ACP this data is actually used — HDMI is a selectable
# profile (COSMIC Settings -> Sound -> Device profiles).
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UCM_CONF=y
BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS=y
# HID + HoG input profiles. Without them bluetoothd has no input profile at all:
# a controller pairs and resolves its GATT HID service, but no uhid/evdev node is
# ever created — the pad keeps blinking, shows only the GATT 50% battery stub and
# never appears in Gamepads. PLUGINS_HID selects PLUGINS_HOG (package Config.in),
# so this covers BLE pads (Xbox, 8BitDo) and classic-BT HIDP pads both. The
# kernel side is already there: CONFIG_BT_HIDP, CONFIG_UHID, CONFIG_HID_MICROSOFT
# (BLUETOOTH ids for the Xbox 1708/1914/1797) and CONFIG_INPUT_JOYDEV.
BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS_PLUGINS_HID=y
# bluetoothctl (the CLI client; selects readline). The COSMIC applet is fine for
# pairing a known-good headset, but it has no way to trust/untrust, no `info`,
# no `monitor`, and no way to say *why* a device failed — which is the whole job
# with unusual hardware. The daemon alone ships no client.
BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS_CLIENT=y
# btmon: the HCI trace. When a device "pairs but doesn't work", this is the only
